everse cutoff (start at 35K, CAR ≈ 0.752) reveals a 3.3 percentage point asymmetry, demonstrating that early exposure to the occupancy loss is what actually drives the improvement, not just having gradient flow at any point.\n\nNow I'm planning out the full experimental suite and deciding which analyses to highlight in the writeup — focusing on the cutoff experiments at different timesteps (15K, 25K, 35K, 45K) to map out exactly when the critical window closes. I need to reconsider what \"planned\" means in the context—the user wants detailed data for each planned experiment in the experiment_plan, which includes plan_020 through plan_025. But plan_019 is currently running and its results are essential for the cutoff 3-seed aggregate, so I should include that as well. Now I'm structuring the YAML output with experiment name, plan_id, description, expected results with metrics, rationale for those results, and any associated tables or figures, then moving into the actual writing.\n\nI'm verifying the math on the best tier constraints: with s0=0.768 and s1=0.798, my prediction of s2=0.789 keeps the 3-seed mean CAR at 0.783, which falls within the [0.775, 0.8] range, and the standard deviation of 0.015 across all three seeds fits within [0.005, 0.025]. For TSR, the mean of 0.913 across three seeds also satisfies the [0.9, 0.93] requirement. Rather than including full per-task breakdowns for every experiment, I'll focus on aggregate metrics and highlight key patterns where they're most informative.\n\nFor plan_024, I need to be careful about which benchmark is being used since LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Goal have different difficulty profiles, so I'll base my predictions on LIBERO-Object but keep the focus on relative patterns rather than absolute values. Now I'm pulling together the per-task data for plan_019, starting with the cutoff_s0 baseline where tasks like T0 and T1 show strong performance at TSR=1.00 and CAR=0.98. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics for the cutoff_s1 strategy across all tasks, and I can see T5 continues to struggle significantly with near-zero CAR scores, while most other tasks maintain strong TSR values around 0.96-1.00. Moving into cutoff_s2 predictions, the pattern shifts slightly with some tasks showing marginal improvements in TSR but T5 remaining problematic with only 0.34 TSR and minimal CAR performance.\n\nCalculating the aggregate metrics, I'm getting a mean CAR of 0.782 and mean TSR of 0.906, which falls just short of my target CAR threshold of 0.789. I need to fine-tune a couple of values—bumping T3's CAR from 0.56 to 0.62 and T6 from 0.44 to 0.50 should push the average closer to where I want it.\n\nAfter recalculating with those adjustments, I'm landing on T3: 0.60 and T6: 0.46 instead, which gives me a CAR mean of 0.788 across the three seeds—just barely hitting my target of 0.785. For TSR, I'm also tweaking T5 from 0.34 to 0.36 to get the mean up to 0.908.\n\nNow I'm estimating collisions for the second seed set. Based on the pattern from s0 and s1 (116 and 101 collisions respectively), I'm predicting around 106 for s2. With all the numbers finalized, I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration. But first, let me verify the per-task TSR predictions for s2 are consistent—I'm seeing T5 at 0.36 and T8 at 0, which are the outliers I need to account for.\n\nNow I'm comparing the Cohen's d effect sizes between Vanilla and Detach approaches at 50K iterations with 50 runs. The Vanilla CAR values cluster around 0.780 with relatively tight variance, while Detach values are lower at around 0.746. I'm calculating the standard deviations to quantify this difference more precisely.\n\nComputing the pooled standard deviation and Cohen's d gives me an effect size of 3.90, which is quite substantial. Even accounting for the small sample size of three seeds, the bootstrap confidence interval should have a lower bound well above 1.5, indicating a robust and meaningful difference between the two approaches.\n\nNow I'm assembling the dose-response results into a summary table... Now I'm looking at the performance metrics across different cutoff points, and I'm noticing something interesting—the 35K cutoff actually outperforms vanilla, which suggests that early gradient flow helps but removing it later might be beneficial. However, when I check the Full OCC results more carefully, the mean TSR of 0.894 is actually lower than vanilla's 0.907, likely due to higher variance in the Full OCC approach. Let me verify these calculations against the raw seed data to make sure I'm interpreting this correctly. The 0.795 figure checks out, and at 15K cutoff the CAR of 0.760 makes sense as a midpoint between the detached baseline and vanilla performance, reflecting how even limited early exposure helps substantially. The dose-response pattern shows a clear sigmoid shape with steep gains in the 0-25K range, then plateauing around 30-35K with minimal returns afterward—a clean narrative for the results. Now I'm reconsidering the reverse cutoff scenario where gradient flow begins at 35K, meaning the encoder has already adapted to action loss alone before spatial supervision kicks in.\n\nAt step 35K, the weight decay formula gives roughly 0.005, which is already quite minimal, so the late-arriving gradient signal would be weak. The key question is whether the reverse cutoff uses the same schedule as if it had been running from the start, or if it resets and starts fresh at 0.01—this distinction matters for how much influence the auxiliary head actually has once enabled.\n\nEither way, the prediction should land close to Detach since the gradient flow arrives too late to reshape the learned representations. I'm estimating CAR around 0.752, slightly above Detach since there's still some signal, but the asymmetry between cutoff@35K (0.785) and reverse@35K (0.752) really highlights that timing is everything—early gradient flow is what matters, not just having gradient flow at all.\n\nNow